{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/8/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bd283c4a\" lines=\"7-23\">\n<description>\nPack all pixels into color-frequency runs along bottom-up columns.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is a rectangular grid whose colors are all foreground material;\nonly the number of pixels of each color matters. In the observed task\ninstances, every present color has a distinct pixel count.\n\nRule\nCount the pixels of each color and order the colors from most frequent to\nleast frequent. Rewrite a same-size grid by placing each color's full count\nas one contiguous run while scanning columns from left to right and, within\neach column, from bottom to top.\n\nKey insight\nThe output preserves the multiset of colors but discards all original\npositions, turning the grid into a frequency-sorted column packing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"465b7d93\" lines=\"97-117\">\n<description>\nMove the small colored sprite into the hollow frame by stretching its shape to the frame interior.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly two foreground colors: one forms a complete rectangular\n  one-pixel-thick frame, and the other forms one 4-connected sprite outside it.\n- The frame interior is initially background.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the original sprite from its outside location.\n- Map the sprite bbox to the frame interior bbox, sending bbox extremes to\n  interior extremes.\n- Draw straight horizontal or vertical segments between mapped positions of\n  adjacent sprite pixels, then fill any enclosed background region inside\n  those drawn segments.\n\nKey insight:\nThe outside sprite is a miniature line/fill diagram for the frame interior,\nnot a bitmap to scale by block replication.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a2d730bd\" lines=\"43-56\">\n<description>\nExtend each solid foreground rectangle toward its same-color isolated pixels with capped one-cell paths, clearing each original marker pixel.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each foreground color has exactly one solid rectangular object and one or more isolated same-color marker pixels.\n- Every marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the rectangle bbox and lies outside it.\nRule\n- For each marker, draw a one-cell-wide straight path from the adjacent side of the rectangle through one cell beyond the marker.\n- Add a three-pixel perpendicular cap at the rectangle end and around the marker endpoint.\n- Recolor the original marker pixel to background.\nKey insight\n- The isolated pixels are not copied; they mark the hollow endpoints of capped protrusions grown from their matching rectangle.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"64efde09\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nCopy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg is the most common color.\n- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.\n- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.\n- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.\n- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c416de3\" lines=\"99-118\">\n<description>\nReplicate a pre-drawn corner pattern to every other marker-bearing corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most frequent color. Hollow 0-frames (possibly partial L-shapes); each\n  frame-corner cell is either 0 or a painted (non-bg, non-0) color.\n- >= 1 'source' corner: the pattern is already drawn (one color, both outward\n  cells and an inward-diagonal trail).\n- Other corners: a single colored pixel on the inward diagonal at the pattern's\n  deepest (k,k) offset; the pixel's color is that corner's target color.\n\nRule:\n- Extract the canonical (TL-oriented) pattern from any source.\n- For each non-source corner whose marker cell is colored, paint the same\n  pattern rotated to that corner's orientation, using the marker's color.\n\nKey insight: the pattern is symmetric about the corner's inward-diagonal axis\n(canonical dr == dc), so it reflects cleanly across all four corner orientations.\nPainting uses each target's OWN marker color, not the source color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cbebaa4b\" lines=\"51-65\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.\n- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.\n\nRule:\n- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.\n- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.\n- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.\n\nKey insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"58c02a16\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nExpand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.\n- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.\n\nRule\n- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.\n- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell\n  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.\n- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.\n\nKey insight\nThe solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the\nseed's background cells are expanded.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\" lines=\"120-136\">\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac0c2ac3\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nFill each centered square ring with the color of the marker at that ring distance.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an odd square grid with a dominant background color and one\nnon-background marker for every Chebyshev distance from the grid center.\nRule\nThe output replaces the background with concentric square rings centered on\nthe middle cell; every cell at distance d from the center takes the color of\nthe unique marker whose distance from the center is d.\nKey insight\nThe scattered markers are not objects to move; their Chebyshev distances\nform a palette for the centered square rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9841fdad\" lines=\"15-39\">\n<description>\nExpand the colored runs from the left reference panel into the blank right panel.\n\nInput structure:\n- A uniform rectangular frame surrounds the grid.\n- One full-height divider of the frame color splits a left reference panel\n  from a blank right panel.\n- Both panels have one background margin column on each side of their active\n  columns; the left active columns contain two-column endpoint slots, possibly\n  connected by same-color horizontal runs.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the original grid and paint only in the right panel's active columns.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '/\"'^def main/,/''^def /p'\"' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8f3a5a89.py | sed '\"'$d'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '/\"'^def main/,/''^def /p'\"' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8f3a5a89.py | sed '\"'$d'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Paint an inner border around the region reachable from a corner marker pixel.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color; wall color (blue) forms barriers dividing the grid.\n    - A unique single pixel (magenta) sits on one corner — the start marker.\n    - Some wall-colored pixels are structural (border-touching, enclosing regions); others are decorations.\n\n    Rule:\n    - Flood-fill from the marker corner to find all reachable bg cells, blocked by structural walls.\n    - Erase wall-colored decorations hidden behind structural walls (not reachable from marker side).\n    - Paint orange (color 7) on every reachable bg cell adjacent to a wall or grid edge — forming a 1-cell inner border.\n\n    Key insight: Only border-connected wall components act as real barriers; interior wall fragments are decorations to be removed.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    h, w = g.shape\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n\n    # Marker pixel is a unique (count==1) non-bg pixel on a corner.\n    counts = Counter(int(v) for v in g.ravel())\n    corners = [(0, 0), (0, w - 1), (h - 1, 0), (h - 1, w - 1)]\n    marker = None\n    for r, c in corners:\n        v = int(g[r, c])\n        if v != bg and counts.get(v, 0) == 1:\n            marker = (r, c, v)\n            break\n    assert marker is not None, \"no unique single-pixel corner marker found\"\n\n    mr, mc, marker_col = marker\n\n    # Wall color: most frequent non-bg, non-marker color.\n    other_cols = [c for c in counts.keys() if c not in (bg, marker_col)]\n    assert other_cols, \"no non-bg non-marker colors found; cannot determine wall color\"\n    wall_col = max(other_cols, key=lambda c: counts[c])\n\n    # First reach: treat all wall pixels as obstacles.\n    reach0 = _flood(\n        (mr, mc),\n        lambda r, c: int(g[r, c]) in (bg, marker_col),\n        h,\n        w,\n    )\n\n    # Structural walls are wall-color components that touch the border AND are adjacent to reach0.\n    walls: set[tuple[int, int]] = set()\n    for comp_arr in components_mask(g == wall_col, connectivity=4):\n        comp = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in comp_arr]\n        touches_border = any(r == 0 or c == 0 or r == h - 1 or c == w - 1 for r, c in comp)\n        if not touches_border:\n            continue\n        adjacent_to_reach0 = any(any((nr, nc) in reach0 for nr, nc in neighbors4(r, c)) for r, c in comp)\n        if adjacent_to_reach0:\n            walls |= set(comp)\n\n    # Second reach: allow passing through non-wall wall-color pixels (decorations) so they don't create borders.\n    reach = _flood(\n        (mr, mc),\n        lambda r, c: int(g[r, c]) in (bg, marker_col) or (int(g[r, c]) == wall_col and (r, c) not in walls),\n        h,\n        w,\n    )\n\n    # Remove wall-colored decorations that are not reachable (they lie behind structural walls).\n    for r in range(h):\n        for c in range(w):\n            if int(out[r, c]) == wall_col and (r, c) not in walls and (r, c) not in reach:\n                out[r, c] = bg\n\n    fill_col = 7\n    # Paint a 1-cell-thick inner border of the reachable region (on background cells only).\n    for r, c in reach:\n        if int(out[r, c]) != bg:\n            continue\n        if r == 0 or c == 0 or r == h - 1 or c == w - 1:\n            out[r, c] = fill_col\n            continue\n        if any((nr, nc) in walls for nr, nc in neighbors8(r, c)):\n            out[r, c] = fill_col\n            continue\n        if any((nr, nc) not in reach for nr, nc in neighbors4(r, c)):\n            out[r, c] = fill_col\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (merged (attempts 1+2 hybrid)) ---------------\n\n# ---- Palette (task colors) ----\nBG = 8\nWALL = 1\nMARKER = 6\nFILL = 7\n\n# ---- Canvas size ----\nGRID_H_RANGE = (10, 20)\nGRID_W_RANGE = (10, 20)\n\n# ---- Structural walls ----\nNUM_STRUCTS_RANGE = (1, 4)\nSTRUCT_SHAPE_RETRIES = 8             # per-shape placement retries\nRECT_MAX_DIM_DIV = 3                 # rect max side ~ dim // 3\nTRI_MAX_SIZE = 6                     # diamond/triangle cap on size\nTRI_MIN_DIM_DIV = 3                  # diamond/top-tri need dim // 3\n\n# ---- Reachable region gate ----\nREACH_RATIO_RANGE = (0.38, 0.87)\n\n# ---- Decorations ----\nNUM_DECOR_RANGE = (0, 3)\nDECOR_AREA_RANGE = (1, 4)            # max pixel-extent of a single decor patch\nDECOR_BORDER = 1                     # stay >= N cells from grid edge\nDECOR_PADDING = 1                    # 8-conn separation from other occupancy\n\n# ---- Outer retry budget ----\nGEN_ATTEMPTS = 300\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# Shape factories (each returns list[(r,c)] or None for a 4-contiguous shape\n# that touches at least one grid edge).\n# =============================================================================\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"实现方案已经定下：\n\n1. `main()` 先从左右边缘检测纵向图例，读取颜色到长度的映射。\n2. 再把图例像素从候选中排除，用 `grey + 图例颜色` 的 4 连通最大直线组件识别主干，支持水平和竖直两种朝向。\n3. 对主干上的每个彩色标记，读取相邻红色方向像素，先清空所有红色，再沿该方向生长同色分支，遇到非背景或边界提前停止。\n4. `generate_input()` 会随机化网格尺寸、图例侧、主干朝向、图例长度、标记数量、颜色选择、分支方向，并通过内部校验避免退化或歧义输入。"}}
